 {
  "pdf_index": 219,
  "blocks": [
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.496002197265625,
     -3.6251630783081055,
     242.05596923828125,
     15.166749954223633
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Semibold",
    "size": 9.166999816894531,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Chapter 10  I  greedy algorithms\n194",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.499996185302734,
     35.11800765991211,
     325.89849853515625,
     140.9711151123047
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "A lot of people tell me that this algorithm seems easy. It’s too obvious, \nso it must be wrong. But that’s the beauty of greedy algorithms: they’re \neasy! A greedy algorithm is simple: at each step, pick the optimal move. \nIn this case, each time you pick a class, you pick the class that ends the \nsoonest. In technical terms, at each step, you pick the locally optimal \nsolution, and in the end, you’re left with the globally optimal solution. \nBelieve it or not, this simple algorithm finds the optimal solution to this \nscheduling problem!",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.499996185302734,
     143.63311767578125,
     316.18768310546875,
     171.47413635253906
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Obviously, greedy algorithms don’t always work. But they’re simple to \nwrite! Let’s look at another example.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     197.19500732421875,
     190.95799255371094,
     219.49700927734375
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Bold",
    "size": 18.0,
    "color": 10690613,
    "text": "The knapsack problem",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     230.61801147460938,
     261.7266540527344,
     284.4630432128906
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Suppose you’re a greedy thief. You’re in a store with  \na knapsack, and there are all these items you can steal.  \nBut you can only take what you can fit in your knapsack.  \nThe knapsack can hold 35 pounds.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     88.69999694824219,
     287.12506103515625,
     319.5965881347656,
     314.966064453125
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "You’re trying to maximize the value of the items you \nput in your knapsack. What algorithm do you use?",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     88.69999694824219,
     317.6280822753906,
     274.6296081542969,
     332.4670715332031
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Again, the greedy strategy is pretty simple:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     88.69999694824219,
     335.1289978027344,
     319.0156555175781,
     362.9700012207031
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "1.   \u0007Pick the most expensive thing that will fit in your \nknapsack.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     88.69999694824219,
     365.6319885253906,
     317.838623046875,
     393.4729919433594
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "2.   \u0007Pick the next most expensive thing that will fit in \nyour knapsack. And so on.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.499992370605469,
     400.6340026855469,
     318.5614929199219,
     428.4750061035156
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Except this time, it doesn’t work! For example, suppose there are three \nitems you can steal.",
    "is_code": false
   }
  ],
  "images": [
   [
