    "size": 9.166999816894531,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Chapter 10  I  greedy algorithms\n196",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     33.74299621582031,
     35.11800765991211,
     318.0654296875,
     88.96305084228516
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "want to see it) and estimate how long it takes. How can you \nmaximize the point total (seeing all the things you really want to \nsee) during your stay? Come up with a greedy strategy. Will that \ngive you the optimal solution?",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.50009536743164,
     91.62503051757812,
     285.2535400390625,
     119.4660415649414
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Let’s look at one last example. This is an example where greedy \nalgorithms are absolutely necessary.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     149.19500732421875,
     214.7882080078125,
     171.49700927734375
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Bold",
    "size": 18.0,
    "color": 10690613,
    "text": "The set-covering problem",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     182.61801147460938,
     280.99859619140625,
     249.46507263183594
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Suppose you’re starting a radio show. You want to \nreach listeners in all 50 US states. You have to decide \nwhat stations to play on to reach all those listeners. It costs \nmoney to be on each station, so you’re trying to minimize the \nnumber of stations you play on. You have a list of stations.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     124.63699340820312,
     252.1270751953125,
     300.9416809082031,
     279.96807861328125
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Each station covers a region, and there’s \noverlap.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     124.63699340820312,
     282.6300964355469,
     306.32073974609375,
     336.4751281738281
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "How do you figure out the smallest set of \nstations you can play on to cover all 50 \nstates? Sounds easy, doesn’t it? Turns out \nit’s extremely hard. Here’s how to do it.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     124.63200378417969,
     339.13714599609375,
     299.55401611328125,
     379.9590148925781
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "List every possible subset of stations. \nThis is called the power set. There are 2n \npossible subsets.",
    "is_code": false
   }
  ],
