     145.46507263183594
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "The problem is that it takes a long time to calculate every possible \nsubset of stations. It takes O(2n) time because there are 2n subsets. It’s \npossible to do if you have a small set of 5 to 10 stations. But with all \nthe examples here, think about what will happen if you have a lot of \nitems. It takes much longer if you have more stations. Suppose you can \ncalculate 10 subsets per second.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.498016357421875,
     148.1270751953125,
     337.91571044921875,
     162.96607971191406
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "There’s no known algorithm that solves it fast enough! What can you do?",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     284.531005859375,
     192.92401123046875,
     301.87701416015625
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Bold",
    "size": 14.0,
    "color": 6513766,
    "text": "Approximation algorithms",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     304.9980163574219,
     337.155517578125,
     332.8390197753906
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Greedy algorithms to the rescue! Here’s a greedy algorithm that comes \npretty close:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     335.5010070800781,
     342.7586975097656,
     376.3440246582031
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "1.\t Pick the station that covers the most states that haven’t been covered \nyet. It’s OK if the station covers some states that have been covered \nalready.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     379.0060119628906,
     207.7789764404297,
     393.84503173828125
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "2.\t Repeat until all the states are covered.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     400.8630065917969,
     334.28778076171875,
     441.8489685058594
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "This is called an approximation algorithm. When calculating the exact \nsolution will take too much time, an approximation algorithm will \nwork. Approximation algorithms are judged by",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     449.0099792480469,
     112.90289306640625,
     463.8489990234375
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "•\t How fast they are",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     471.010009765625,
     222.108642578125,
     485.8489990234375
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "•\t How close they are to the optimal solution",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     488.5110168457031,
     335.2535095214844,
     542.3390502929688
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Greedy algorithms are a good choice because not only are they simple \nto come up with, but that simplicity means they usually run fast, too. \nIn this case, the greedy algorithm runs in O(n2) time, where n is the \nnumber of radio stations.",
    "is_code": false
   }
  ],
  "images": [
   [
    128.39100646972656,
    175.697998046875,
    242.63101196289062,
    267.37701416015625
   ]
  ]
 },
 {
  "pdf_index": 223,
  "blocks": [
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.496002197265625,
     -3.6251630783081055,
     242.05596923828125,
     15.166749954223633
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Semibold",
    "size": 9.166999816894531,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Chapter 10  I  greedy algorithms\n198",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     35.11800765991211,
     187.61973571777344,
     49.957008361816406
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Let’s see how this problem looks in code.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     61.65100860595703,
     100.2480239868164,
     78.99700927734375
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Bold",
    "size": 14.0,
    "color": 6513766,
    "text": "Code for setup",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     82.11801147460938,
     323.2144775390625,
     109.95902252197266
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "For this example, I’m going to use a subset of the states and the stations \nto keep things simple.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     112.62103271484375,
     218.77166748046875,
     127.46002960205078
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "First, make a list of the states you want to cover:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     18.5,
     133.59600830078125,
     323.4757995605469,
     154.37200927734375
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "states_needed = set([\"mt\", \"wa\", \"or\", \"id\", \"nv\", \"ut\",\n\"ca\", \"az\"])   \nYou pass an array in, and it gets converted to a set.",
    "is_code": true
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.499984741210938,
     163.61801147460938,
     327.37139892578125,
     204.46104431152344
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "I used a set for this. A set is like a list, except that each item can show up \nonly once in a set. Sets can’t have duplicates. For example, suppose you \nhad this list:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     18.5,
     212.12864685058594,
     161.1814422607422,
     221.12864685058594
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": ">>> arr = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]",
    "is_code": true
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     226.11801147460938,
     138.27476501464844,
     240.95701599121094
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "And you converted it to a set:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     18.5,
     248.62864685058594,
     85.85871887207031,
     267.6276550292969
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": ">>> set(arr)\nset([1, 2, 3])",
    "is_code": true
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     277.1180114746094,
     244.5545654296875,
     291.9570007324219
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "The numbers 1, 2, and 3 all show up just once in a set.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     356.6579895019531,
     314.8433837890625,
     384.4989929199219
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "You also need the list of stations that you’re choosing from. I chose to \nuse a dict for this:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     18.5,
     392.16864013671875,
     236.84072875976562,
     451.16363525390625
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "stations = {}\nstations[\"kone\"] = set([\"id\", \"nv\", \"ut\"])\nstations[\"ktwo\"] = set([\"wa\", \"id\", \"mt\"])\nstations[\"kthree\"] = set([\"or\", \"nv\", \"ca\"])\nstations[\"kfour\"] = set([\"nv\", \"ut\"])\nstations[\"kfive\"] = set([\"ca\", \"az\"])",
    "is_code": true
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     460.6579895019531,
     319.8913879394531,
     514.5029907226562
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "The keys are station names, and the values are the states they cover. \nSo, in this example, the kone station covers Idaho, Nevada, and Utah. \nAll the values are sets, too. Making everything a set will make your life \neasier, as you’ll see soon.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     9.5,
     517.1649780273438,
     313.908447265625,
     532.0040283203125
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Finally, you need something to hold the final set of stations you’ll use:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     18.5,
     539.6686401367188,
     134.27236938476562,
     548.6686401367188
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "final_stations = set()",
    "is_code": true
   }
  ],
  "images": [
   [
    19.31100082397461,
    302.6969909667969,
    315.7120056152344,
    346.5369873046875
   ]
  ]
 },
 {
  "pdf_index": 224,
  "blocks": [
   {
    "bbox": [
     140.05299377441406,
     0.14801597595214844,
     432.5,
     11.166749954223633
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-It",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "The set-covering problem\n199",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     34.65399932861328,
     169.09600830078125,
     52.0
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Bold",
    "size": 14.0,
    "color": 6513766,
    "text": "Calculating the answer",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     55.12099838256836,
     313.2634582519531,
     95.96402740478516
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Now you need to calculate what stations you’ll use. Take a look \nat the image at right, and see if you can predict what stations you \nshould use.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     98.62600708007812,
     294.65252685546875,
     139.4690399169922
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "There can be more than one correct solution. You need to go \nthrough every station and pick the one that covers the most \nuncovered states. I’ll call this best_station:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     36.5,
     147.1316375732422,
     308.0621032714844,
     177.66598510742188
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "best_station = None\nstates_covered = set()\nfor station, states_for_station in stations.items():",
    "is_code": true
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.495994567871094,
     185.62100219726562,
     340.72332763671875,
     252.4640350341797
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "states_covered is the biggest set of states we can newly cover. \nRemember, we are trying to find the station that will cover the \nmost states that haven’t been covered yet. The for loop allows you to \nloop over every station to see which one is the best station. Let’s look at \nthe body of the for loop:",
    "is_code": true
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     36.5,
     260.13165283203125,
     288.2621765136719,
     299.1286315917969
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "covered = states_needed & states_for_station   \nif len(covered) > len(states_covered):\n  best_station = station\n  states_covered = covered",
    "is_code": true
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     308.6210021972656,
     173.61077880859375,
     323.4599914550781
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "There’s a funny-looking line here:",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     36.5,
     331.13165283203125,
     272.06219482421875,
     340.13165283203125
    ],
    "font": "Courier",
    "size": 9.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "covered = states_needed & states_for_station",
    "is_code": true
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     345.1210021972656,
     102.23400115966797,
     359.9599914550781
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "What’s going on?",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     371.65399169921875,
     53.76399612426758,
     389.0
    ],
    "font": "MyriadPro-Bold",
    "size": 14.0,
    "color": 6513766,
    "text": "Sets",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     392.1210021972656,
     167.4397430419922,
     406.9599914550781
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "Suppose you have a set of fruits.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     479.6260070800781,
     169.7717742919922,
     494.4649963378906
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "You also have a set of vegetables.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     27.5,
     532.1289672851562,
     310.16265869140625,
     546.968017578125
    ],
    "font": "MinionPro-Regular",
    "size": 11.0,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "When you have two sets, you can do some fun things with them.",
    "is_code": false
   },
   {
    "bbox": [
     335.7550048828125,
     261.4649963378906,
     422.3050231933594,
     280.37176513671875
    ],
    "font": "Univers-Bold",
    "size": 7.5,
    "color": 2301728,
    "text": "New syntax! This is \ncalled a set intersection.",
    "is_code": false
   }
  ],
